  start:{lede:"You never need the slides: everything for the test is taught here, step by step. Everything in this lecture can be tested (except boxes marked <i>extra</i>). The test is the 5-minute quiz at the start of Monday's lecture (16 Nov).",status:"Next test: start of the lecture on Monday 16 Nov. It covers this lecture.",plan:"<h3>Tonight, about 2 hours</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li><b>Learn, step by step</b>: 9 short steps. Do every check question and play with each widget.</li>\n      <li><b>12 layer cards</b>: flip all 12, then play \"Which one am I?\" until you get 8 right in a row.</li>\n      <li>Sleep. Sleep is when it sticks.</li>\n    </ol>\n    <h3>Before the test, 1 to 2 hours</h3>\n    <ol>\n      <li><b>Rocks &amp; numbers</b>: do the \"Which layer?\" drill until you get 10 in a row.</li>\n      <li><b>Vocabulary</b> quiz, then <b>Cheat sheet</b> in test mode: say each blurred answer out loud, then tap to check.</li>\n      <li><b>Mock test</b> (7 questions, 5 minutes) twice, then <b>Review</b> until it is empty.</li>\n    </ol>",six:["<b>Chemical layers (3)</b>, by composition: <b>crust</b> (silicate, basaltic to granitic), <b>mantle</b> (peridotite, ultramafic), <b>core</b> (Fe-Ni alloy).", "<b>Physical layers (5)</b>, by behaviour: <b>lithosphere</b> (rigid) → <b>asthenosphere</b> (plastic) → <b>mesosphere</b> (more rigid) → <b>outer core</b> (liquid) → <b>inner core</b> (rigid).", "<b>Moho</b> = crust/mantle, about <b>5 to 70 km</b>. <b>Gutenberg</b> = mantle/core, about <b>2900 km</b>. Earth's radius <b>6378 km</b>.", "<b>Oceanic crust</b>: mid-ocean ridges, basalt + gabbro, thin (5 to 7 km), dense (~3 g/cm³), young (up to ~200 million years). <b>Continental crust</b>: all rock types, thick (~35 km), buoyant (~2.7 g/cm³), old (up to billions of years).", "<b>Drivers</b>: heat (solar = external; primordial and radiogenic K, Th, U = internal), gravity, rotation. Internal heat → <b>endogenous</b>, solar heat → <b>exogenous</b>.", "<b>How we know</b>: rocks (outcrops, drilling, mining, xenoliths, ophiolites), meteorites, lab experiments, modelling; and for the physical layers <b>seismology</b> and the <b>magnetic field</b> (geodynamo in the liquid outer core)."]},
